#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134636} :activity/id #uuid "600046de-0665-50c3-a999-1a23cc41313b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050993 :topic/id #uuid "77b8e530-6866-509a-8397-d35f1b4f5874" :topic/math-academy-id 2097} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135034 :tutorial/content "The standard inner product of $\\Bbb R^n,$ i.e. the dot product, does not meet the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFor example, if $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is a valid inner product on $\\Bbb C^n,$ then for any vector $\\mathbf a \\in \\Bbb C^n,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathbf a, \\, \\mathbf a\\rangle \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nBut for many vectors in $\\Bbb C^n,$ such as $[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ in $\\Bbb C^2,$ the dot product fails to satisfy this criterion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & ={\\text{i}}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThankfully, there is an elegant way to generalize the dot product to satisfy the desired criterion on $\\Bbb C^n.$ We can just take the complex conjugate of each element in the right vector before carrying out the dot product.\n\nMore formally, for two vectors $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in \\mathbb C^n$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe define the **standard Hermitian inner product** as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{{y}_{i}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{y_i}$ is the complex conjugate of $y_i.$\n\nUsing the standard Hermitian inner product instead of the dot product, our desired criterion is satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]⟩ & =\\text{i}\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,\\text{i}\\,}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,0\\,} \\\\  & =\\text{i}\\cdot (-\\text{i})+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ge 0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be verified that the Hermitian inner product satisfies *all* the criteria of an inner product on $\\Bbb C^n.$\n\nFurthermore, since the complex conjugate leaves real numbers unchanged, the Hermitian inner product simplifies to the dot product on $\\Bbb R^n.$ In other words, the dot product is just a special case of the Hermitian inner product!" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca70c748-2628-5d2d-b0dc-ab7a0675355a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3605 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5959b5ca-9405-5f0f-b4d5-5a7a8e442cfc" :step/math-academy-id 20193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133765 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133574 :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048861}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360718}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "1f2f6fb1-f106-52c7-b55a-201253d4ed07" :step/math-academy-id 20194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133575 :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360785}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "f393944b-d78a-5d8a-aa1a-c7c5133f9717" :step/math-academy-id 20196 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135100 :tutorial/content "A Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is **linear in the first argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is **antilinear in the second argument.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has **conjugate symmetry.** For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is **positive-definite.** For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44cf93d3-a069-54ee-bfbf-93d36fd338cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5088 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Inner Product Over the Complex Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "f16cfe03-2cd2-5567-81c0-5f200f0b4b5f" :step/math-academy-id 31867 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133865 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133681 :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360771}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"} :step/id #uuid "418336f6-44aa-5629-a383-cc4e0867f9c9" :step/math-academy-id 31868}] :activity/title "The Inner Product in Vector Spaces Over the Complex Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133984}]}
